Samsung has been offering wireless  earbuds for a while now  even before the earpods but they never  really took off the battery life was  horrible they had some connection issues  and then the price well about a month  ago they fixed all of that and for our  one-month review of the Galaxy buds I'll  have to say that they're great and for  many they're actually better than the  air pods now don't get me wrong  air pods are excellent but there are a  few ways the galaxy pods simply do it  better the first and most important  thing is the price you're paying 130  dollars compared to 200 for the air pods  - and that could be a deal-breaker for a  lot of people Samsung had to price these  competitively and they did they're  mainly marketed to Android users but  some iPhone users may even choose to buy  these over-the-air pods - so we tested  the Galaxy buds with an iPhone and made  a video on it to see if they're worth it  for iPhone users of course they're a lot  better with an Android phone because you  can download the dedicated Galaxy buds  app which I'll get into in just a minute  back to the air pots - you could say 40  dollars by not getting the wireless  charging case but the Galaxy buds have  that feature included for the same price  and the great thing is that Samsung's  s10 line of smartphones all support  reverse and wireless charging so you can  charge these without a cable  realistically you won't always use this  but it's nice to know that it's there  just in case you need it another great  thing about the galaxy bus is that they  seal in your ears so while they don't  have active noise cancellation they  still pluck out a good amount of  background noise the air paws feature an  open design so you still hear everything  in the background and when you turn the  volume up people all around you can more  easily hear what you're listening to if  you're using the galaxy buds with  Android you'll have the ambient aware  mode and can tweak the sound profile  this is missing on iOS but the default  settings sounded best to us  Samsung also added the Apple like  automatic sync setup but it's not as  simple because of all the permission  requests you have to accept the seal  design of the Galaxy buds gives you a  lot more privacy and it also makes them  sound louder than the speakers actually  are since all the sound is sealed into  your ear and can't escape this greatly  increases bass reproduction and it  actually helps them save battery life  since the speakers themselves aren't as  loud  they're also splash and sweat resistant  just make sure not to get any water into  the speaker grille on the inside another  thing I like about the Galaxy buds is  just how subtle they are they sit flush  in your ears with nothing hanging out or  wrapping around your ear and it's  actually quite impressive how will they  fit and stay in your ears and I  absolutely love that Samsung Desai  not to put their logo on the face of the  buds so they look really clean each bud  has a touch surface that you could  customize and overall it works quite  well you can even change bixie out for  google assistant they come with a  charging case that's a bit larger  overall than the earpods and it has less  battery life seven hours of additional  juice instead of round 19 now that's a  big difference on paper but not as big  in the real world unless you use them  all day long for most you can plug them  in to charge for an hour every three to  four days or so and you'll be fine for  the buzz themselves Samsung rates the  Galaxy buds at around six hours while  Apple rates the air pots at five take a  look at the sound guys truly wireless  test the Galaxy buds are leading the  pack with six and a half hours while the  air pots come in at just over four hours  that's a lot of positives for the Galaxy  buds so what are the downsides the  biggest one for consumers is the volume  for most quality content they are loud  enough because of the seal and the sound  quality is great for the price but I  really wish they were louder the air  pots can get loud too loud actually but  that comes in handy when the podcast or  video you're listening to is too quiet  with the air pods you have more Headroom  to compensate but in cases like this the  Galaxy buds can be a little hard to use  my other complaints is the mic quality  it's usable but doesn't sound great  here's a comparison this is a microphone  quality test with the Galaxy buds this  is a microphone quality tests with the  air pods to the air pots mic is closer  and pointed at your mouth so you can get  much better mic quality instead of  having the mic flush with your head  which makes it harder to pick up your  speech another limitation compared to  the new air pods is no hands-free access  to your virtual assistant this isn't a  big deal for me personally but let me  know if it is for you down in the  comment section below overall just like  with sm+ and s10 II I'm really impressed  with the Galaxy buds now they're not  perfect but considering the sound  quality convenience features and price  they are an excellent value even if  you're an iPhone user this have been  Mac's with Mac stack if you want to see  more videos like this one click that  little circle above and check out our  galaxy buds for iPhone users video we're  right over here thanks for watching and  we'll see you in the next one
 

